{"version":1,"slug":"seasons","topic":"Why do we have seasons?","level":"beginner","title":"Why Do We Have Seasons?","summary":"You will understand how Earth's tilt—not its distance from the sun—creates the seasons and why they are opposite in each hemisphere.","scopeNote":null,"scenes":[{"id":"intro","kind":"intro","title":"Why do we have seasons?","segments":[{"id":"intro-0","narration":"Good question. Why do we have seasons? Let's figure it out together.","sig":"v8mq_Vr_qxAfc3S1YEGOz12z5iQFgvs4","actions":[]},{"id":"intro-1","narration":"In January, Australia is sweltering in summer while New York is freezing in winter. Why does the same sun heat us so differently?","sig":"WoLrH5b-pduYarA4KgW5ipAx3ZPJEQyl","actions":[{"id":"hero","kind":"icon","cue":"In January, Australia","text":"Our tilted Earth","items":null,"icon":"globe","at":{"col":0,"row":1,"w":4,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"intro-2","narration":"Here's our plan. First, The Tilt, Not Distance. Then, How Sunlight Hits Earth. And finally, Opposite Seasons.","sig":"W5qe0-0MBRhkLHibrz__omDyWGKlaMF9","actions":[{"id":"agenda","kind":"timeline","cue":"Here's our plan","text":null,"items":["1: The Tilt, Not Distance","2: How Sunlight Hits Earth","3: Opposite Seasons"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":5,"row":1,"w":7,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c1","kind":"chapter","title":"The Tilt, Not Distance","segments":[{"id":"c1-0","narration":"Many people think summer happens because Earth gets closer to the sun. That sounds logical, like standing closer to a campfire.","sig":"NQCkmjlyJGela6TqpPVhjowAyh_L23l7","actions":[{"id":"wrong","kind":"box","cue":"summer happens because","text":"Closer = Summer","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":7,"row":0,"w":4,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"red","expr":null},{"id":"sun","kind":"icon","cue":"to the sun","text":"Sun","items":null,"icon":"sun","at":{"col":1,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-1","narration":"But Earth's orbit is nearly a circle, and we are actually closest to the sun in January! So distance is not the reason.","sig":"9rtlzK2raycr-mX8VcS9CRv0docFLxbv","actions":[{"id":"earth","kind":"icon","cue":"Earth's orbit is","text":"Earth","items":null,"icon":"earth","at":{"col":4,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"orb","kind":"orbit","cue":"nearly a circle","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":"sun","target":"earth","color":null,"expr":null},{"id":"x1","kind":"cross_out","cue":"not the reason","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"wrong","color":"red","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-2","narration":"Instead, Earth is tilted on its side by about twenty three and a half degrees as it travels around the sun.","sig":"QeA1fa11KLSmNE1_qFqlDo9FVFCOve4G","actions":[{"id":"tilt","kind":"sketch","cue":"tilted on its side","text":"23.5° Tilt","items":["fill blue circle 50 50 28","line 62 12 38 88","arc 50 50 38 270 293"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":7,"row":2,"w":3,"h":3},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"chk","kind":"check","cue":"twenty three and","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"tilt","color":"green","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-3","narration":"That tilt is the real secret behind every season.","sig":"bCcxB3n8mcmWDItxPNl7xSFdl3kquK2w","actions":[{"id":"p1","kind":"pulse","cue":"the real secret","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"tilt","color":"green","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c2","kind":"chapter","title":"How Sunlight Hits Earth","segments":[{"id":"c2-0","narration":"So if Earth's tilt is the real reason, how does it actually change the temperature? In summer, your part of Earth tilts toward the sun, so sunlight hits the ground straight on.","sig":"ZI_EG-N3-xNDTTAL4s0OdO-LJf9JBry9","actions":[{"id":"summer_sketch","kind":"sketch","cue":"hits the ground straight","text":"Summer: Direct Rays","items":["line 10 85 90 85","fill orange rect 35 80 30 6","orange arrow 40 10 40 75","orange arrow 50 10 50 75","orange arrow 60 10 60 75"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":0,"row":0,"w":4,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"summer_box","kind":"box","cue":"straight on","text":"Concentrated heat","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":0,"row":4,"w":4,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-1","narration":"In winter, your part tilts away, so the exact same amount of sunlight arrives at a low, slanted angle. It spreads out over a much bigger area, so each spot gets less heat.","sig":"3-j1mXmosm8n_BNOvqP6m96FTOVstOXh","actions":[{"id":"winter_sketch","kind":"sketch","cue":"low, slanted angle","text":"Winter: Slanted Rays","items":["line 10 85 90 85","fill blue rect 15 80 70 6","blue arrow 10 20 30 75","blue arrow 25 20 50 75","blue arrow 40 20 70 75"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":4,"row":0,"w":4,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"winter_box","kind":"box","cue":"spreads out over","text":"Spread-out heat","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":4,"row":4,"w":4,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-2","narration":"Plus, that tilt gives you longer days in summer and shorter days in winter, which means more hours of sunshine to warm things up.","sig":"QZv5bCcxCfbLZrRhYVjQYRR_05EBFTYv","actions":[{"id":"day_note","kind":"note","cue":"longer days in summer","text":"Longer summer days = more warming time","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":8,"row":1,"w":4,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"purple","expr":null},{"id":"pulse_summer","kind":"pulse","cue":"warm things up","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"summer_box","color":"orange","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c3","kind":"chapter","title":"Opposite Seasons","segments":[{"id":"c3-0","narration":"Now that you know how Earth's tilt changes sunlight, let's see what that means for the whole planet. Because Earth is tilted, its two halves always get opposite amounts of sun.","sig":"YNbgbtjYS2L_20ftEDR_mv7wtFjvzLVE","actions":[{"id":"globe","kind":"icon","cue":"for the whole planet","text":"Two Halves","items":null,"icon":"globe","at":{"col":5,"row":0,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"p1","kind":"pulse","cue":"two halves always get","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"globe","color":"orange","expr":null}]},{"id":"c3-1","narration":"In June, the Northern Hemisphere tilts toward the sun and enjoys warm summer days. At the exact same time, the Southern Hemisphere tilts away, so it is winter there.","sig":"K2Li8hUKVRAkgF4fBmpDnx36xCuPgb07","actions":[{"id":"table","kind":"compare","cue":"In June, the Northern","text":null,"items":["Time of Year | Northern Hemisphere | Southern Hemisphere","June | Summer | Winter","December | Winter | Summer"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":1,"row":2,"w":10,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"ink","expr":null},{"id":"c1","kind":"callout","cue":"tilts away, so it","text":"Opposite tilt, opposite season","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"table","color":"orange","expr":null}]},{"id":"c3-2","narration":"Six months later in December, Earth is on the other side of the sun, so everything flips. Now the South gets summer while the North bundles up for winter.","sig":"B1XZQRy8OPWNp9ikjlXNwfddOnwz0oD9","actions":[{"id":"ch1","kind":"check","cue":"so everything flips","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"table","color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"u1","kind":"underline","cue":"bundles up for winter","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"globe","color":"green","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"Recap","segments":[{"id":"recap-0","narration":"So remember: Earth's tilt causes the seasons, not distance. That tilt changes how directly sunlight hits us, giving opposite seasons to each half. What questions do you have?","sig":"swckMiujIGi-XxgR-gwL-BcWMNjc-Huw","actions":[{"id":"recap_list","kind":"list","cue":"Earth's tilt causes","text":null,"items":["Tilt causes seasons, not distance","Direct sunlight brings warmer days","North and South have opposite seasons"],"icon":"circle-check","at":{"col":1,"row":1,"w":6,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"recap_globe","kind":"icon","cue":"opposite seasons","text":"Tilted Earth","items":null,"icon":"globe","at":{"col":8,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]}]}],"quiz":[{"question":"Why is it warmer in summer than in winter?","options":["Earth is closer to the sun","Earth's tilt points us toward the sun","The sun burns hotter in July","The moon blocks less heat"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Earth's tilt—not its distance from the sun—is what causes the seasons.","chapter":1},{"question":"Why does slanted winter sunlight feel cooler than direct summer sunlight?","options":["It travels through more space","Clouds absorb all the heat","It spreads out over a larger area","The sun shrinks in winter"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Slanted sunlight spreads the same amount of energy over a wider area, so each spot gets less heat.","chapter":2},{"question":"When it is summer in the Northern Hemisphere, what season is it in the Southern Hemisphere?","options":["Winter","Summer","Spring","Autumn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because Earth is tilted, the two hemispheres always experience opposite seasons.","chapter":3}],"suggestions":["Why do days get longer?","How do moon phases work?","What causes ocean tides?"],"models":{"lesson":"google/gemini-4-hydrogen","voice":"google/gemini-3.8-flash-tts"},"createdAt":"2026-11-17T17:38:26.320Z","audioBase":"/samples/seasons"}